{
  "ResearchDossier": {
    "topic": "the history of artificial intelligence",
    "summary": "AI was named in a 1955 proposal for a two-month summer study at Dartmouth (1956). Hype (the Navy's 1958 perceptron), early chatbots (ELIZA, 1966), two AI winters, then the 2017 transformer paper led to ChatGPT in 2022.",
    "angle": "Artificial intelligence was planned as a two-month summer project; it has been running for 70 years.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "id": "c1",
        "text": "On 2 September 1955 John McCarthy, Marvin Minsky, Nathaniel Rochester and Claude Shannon proposed 'a 2-month, 10-man study of artificial intelligence' for summer 1956 at Dartmouth, arguing a significant advance could be made if scientists worked on it together for a summer.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dartmouth_workshop",
            "title": "Dartmouth workshop - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "We propose that a 2-month, 10-man study of artificial intelligence be carried out during the summer of 1956"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c2",
        "text": "The proposal is credited with introducing the term 'artificial intelligence'; the 1956 Dartmouth workshop is widely considered the founding event of AI as a field.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dartmouth_workshop",
            "title": "Dartmouth workshop - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "the founding event of artificial intelligence as a field"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c3",
        "text": "At a 1958 press conference organised by the US Navy, Frank Rosenblatt presented the perceptron; The New York Times called it the embryo of a computer the Navy expected to walk, talk, see, write, reproduce itself and be conscious of its existence.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perceptron",
            "title": "Perceptron - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "the embryo of an electronic computer that [the Navy] expects will be able to walk, talk, see, write"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c4",
        "text": "ELIZA was developed from 1964 to 1967 at MIT by Joseph Weizenbaum (released 1966); its DOCTOR script simulated a Rogerian psychotherapist.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ELIZA",
            "title": "ELIZA - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "developed from 1964 to 1967 at MIT by Joseph Weizenbaum"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c5",
        "text": "Weizenbaum's secretary asked him to leave the room so that she and ELIZA could have a real conversation.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ELIZA",
            "title": "ELIZA - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "asked Weizenbaum to leave the room so that she and ELIZA could have a real conversation"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c6",
        "text": "AI winters are periods of reduced funding and interest in AI research; the two major ones lasted roughly 1974–1980 and 1987–2000.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AI_winter",
            "title": "AI winter - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "an AI winter is a period of reduced funding and interest in AI research"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c7",
        "text": "'Attention Is All You Need' (2017), by eight researchers at Google, introduced the transformer, which underlies most modern large language models.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attention_Is_All_You_Need",
            "title": "Attention Is All You Need - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "introduced a new deep learning architecture known as the transformer"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      },
      {
        "id": "c8",
        "text": "ChatGPT, released by OpenAI on 30 November 2022 (GPT: generative pre-trained transformer), gained 100 million users in two months.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ChatGPT",
            "title": "ChatGPT - Wikipedia",
            "quote": "gained one million users in five days and 100 million in two months"
          }
        ],
        "confidence": "high"
      }
    ],
